-- title:
--   Computing a compact downset address from a mask
-- statement:
--   Let $m<2^L$ be a mask with exactly $k$ set bits. Its compact downset address is the number of smaller cardinality subsets plus its position among the $k$-subsets ordered by increasing binary mask:
--   $$\rho_L(m)=\sum_{i<k}\binom Li+\operatorname{rank}_{L,k}(m).$$
--   Assume the specified subset-setup and layer-offset procedures are present. With scratch base $w$ and machine limits $S,W,D$, require
--   $$w+(L+1)^2+(L+1)\le S\le W,\quad(L+1)2^{L+1}\le W,\quad d+L+4\le D.$$
--   Starting from arbitrary memory, the procedure returns $\rho_L(m)$ within $23L^2+286L+284$ steps and preserves every address below $w$.
--
--   This supplies a compact subset address while constructing its own Pascal and powers tables.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightDownsetAddress.lean#L26-L71

